  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nGlobal models in the mid-range continue to place the H500 high \npressure center over the Texas and Louisiana gulf coast, while the\naxis of the ridge extends into portions of the central Great \nPlains. Favorable steering flow on the windward side of the upper \nlevel high will allow our CWA to experience back-to-back rain \nchances this week. With a deepening trough expected to traverse \nacross the Atlantic coastal regions late this week, this may \ntrigger another subtle western transition from the upper level \nhigh. If the ridge does reposition more over our area or due \nsouth, expect surface temperatures to rise and rain chances to \ndecline next weekend. \n\nFor the rest of this week, highs look to remain in the 70s and 80s\nacross the CWA. PoPs in the extended period currently favor the\nwestern Panhandles more than the rest of the area. The main \nconcern will be the extent of thunderstorm coverage each day. If \nstorm mode is more conducive for scattered thunderstorms as \nopposed to widespread thunderstorms, we can expect a decline in \nprecipitation chances as models resolve where thunderstorms may \nsetup spatially.\n\nRangel",
